                  <div class="text-2xl mb-2">🪣</div>
                  <h5 class="font-bold text-steel-800 mb-1">Variable ①: Barrel diameter = per-batch capacity</h5>
                  <p class="text-sm text-steel-700 leading-relaxed"><b class="text-amber-600">390 mm standard barrel</b> ≤ 100 kg / batch, default configuration, higher throughput. <b class="text-amber-600">300 mm small barrel</b> ≤ 60 kg / batch but concentrates cake pressure, ideal for extra-hard seeds (copra, hemp). <b>Barrel diameter is an independent option — every model can be ordered with 390 mm or 300 mm barrel.</b> Higher model numbers do NOT change per-batch capacity.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-red-50 rounded-xl p-5 border-l-4 border-red-500">
                  <div class="text-2xl mb-2">💪</div>
                  <h5 class="font-bold text-steel-800 mb-1">Variable ②: Real tonnage = how much force</h5>
                  <p class="text-sm text-steel-700 leading-relaxed">More real tonnage → <b class="text-red-600">able to press harder seeds</b> and extract oil more completely (lower residual oil in cake). Soft seeds (peanut, rapeseed, sesame) 300–400 real tons sufficient. Hard seeds (copra, hemp, walnut) need 500+ tons. <b>Simple rule: the harder the seed, the larger the model number you need.</b></p>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-green-50 rounded-xl p-5 border-l-4 border-green-600">
                  <div class="text-2xl mb-2">📦</div>
                  <h5 class="font-bold text-steel-800 mb-1">Variable ③: Single vs Double Barrel = reduce idle time</h5>
                  <p class="text-sm text-steel-700 leading-relaxed">Any model can be ordered in a <b class="text-green-600">double-barrel</b> version. This does NOT press two batches at once — instead, one barrel presses while you <b>load/unload the other</b>. When the first barrel finishes, you switch immediately, eliminating wait time between batches. So double-barrel <b>does not change batch size or press time, but increases the number of batches per day</b>. It makes the machine larger, heavier and more expensive. <b>Simple rule: if you need large daily output, go double-barrel.</b></p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </section>

